WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.267
-I'm so happy to be able
to talk to you,

00:00:02.267 --> 00:00:03.234
and thank you for being here.

00:00:03.234 --> 00:00:04.467
I have a lot of questions
for you.

00:00:04.467 --> 00:00:06.734
Congrats on the self-titled
album, by the way.

00:00:06.734 --> 00:00:08.000
-Thank you, thank you.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.667
-I know you were just at
Coachella with Anitta,

00:00:10.667 --> 00:00:12.167
who has been on the show.
She's awesome.

00:00:12.167 --> 00:00:15.067
What even is that scene
remotely like?

00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:16.934
I'm not saying I would never go,

00:00:16.934 --> 00:00:19.067
but I just don't know
if I'd fit in there.

00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:20.534
-You would love it.
You would love it.

00:00:20.534 --> 00:00:22.200
And you know Anitta. I know she
was here a couple weeks ago.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:23.667
-She's amazing. So fun.

00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:26.033
-She has, like, more followers
than, like, the state of Iowa.

00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:29.100
She's one of the most popular --
-She's got so much energy.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.200
-She works harder than anybody
else.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.300
And I think when people talk
about that weekend in Coachella,

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.200
she was the one thing people
talked about the most

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:36.667
'cause she brought
this Brazilian culture

00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:38.501
and did everything there.

00:00:38.501 --> 00:00:40.400
Had me out to deejay with her,
but it was --

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.434
Being on main stage with her was
something I'll remember.

00:00:42.434 --> 00:00:43.467
It was amazing.

00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:45.367
-You're also closing Stagecoach.

00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:47.501
-Right, yeah.
That's this weekend.

00:00:47.501 --> 00:00:49.567
-Which is a huge --
It's a country music special.

00:00:49.567 --> 00:00:53.167
-Yeah.
-So, how do those crowds differ?

00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:54.601
-They're totally different.

00:00:54.601 --> 00:00:56.767
I think Stagecoach is a little
bit smaller than Coachella.

00:00:56.767 --> 00:01:00.868
Coachella is two weekends and
there's like 40 levels of VIP.

00:01:00.868 --> 00:01:02.834
You have general admission
and you have all the way to

00:01:02.834 --> 00:01:04.601
the craziest artist passes.

00:01:04.601 --> 00:01:07.400
And it's almost like influencer
survivor there,

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.567
like to figure out
where you belong

00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:09.801
and how to get into parties.

00:01:09.801 --> 00:01:11.601
-Influencer survivor?
-Yeah.

00:01:11.601 --> 00:01:13.667
It's pretty hard-core,
if you know what I'm saying.

00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:15.901
-To see who makes it
to the next level, yeah.

00:01:15.901 --> 00:01:17.334
-It's kind of crazy.

00:01:17.334 --> 00:01:19.300
But Stagecoach is
a more mellower festival.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.734
It's country.
-Do you like doing country?

00:01:21.734 --> 00:01:24.267
-I've been loving it.
I did a country album about

00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:27.167
two years ago called
"Thomas Wesley Presents."

00:01:27.167 --> 00:01:29.767
And I did a couple songs
that were really big.

00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:32.467
And the album went gold.
And I'm going to do another one,

00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:33.834
I think, this year, so --

00:01:33.834 --> 00:01:35.634
-They're amazing fans,
country music fans.

00:01:35.634 --> 00:01:37.167
We love everything here.

00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:38.634
-Yeah, Luke Combs is
playing Stagecoach.

00:01:38.634 --> 00:01:40.601
And I'm playing after him
at the Palomino tent,

00:01:40.601 --> 00:01:42.534
like a deejay set,
kind of mixing country

00:01:42.534 --> 00:01:44.334
and house music and dance.

00:01:44.334 --> 00:01:46.667
-Do you get nervous
when you do these?

00:01:46.667 --> 00:01:48.467
-The last time I did Stagecoach,

00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:50.801
I spent the afternoon
with Guy Fieri.

00:01:50.801 --> 00:01:52.834
And I drank an entire bottle of
mescal.

00:01:52.834 --> 00:01:54.634
And I really like --
[ Laughter ]

00:01:54.634 --> 00:01:56.501
-That's how it is hanging out
with Guy Fieri, man.

00:01:56.501 --> 00:01:57.834
-And I was at the stage
and I was like --

00:01:57.834 --> 00:01:59.000
I literally looked at
the turntables

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.400
and I really forgot
how they worked.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:03.400
-Oh, my God.
-But something happened.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.601
It was magic and I just kind of
got the set together.

00:02:05.601 --> 00:02:07.334
We brought Lil Nas X out with
Billy Ray Cyrus,

00:02:07.334 --> 00:02:09.267
the first time he performed.
-That's right.

00:02:09.267 --> 00:02:11.334
-The whole crowd was like -- It
was like a really amazing moment

00:02:11.334 --> 00:02:13.434
'cause country and rap had
this crossover aspect

00:02:13.434 --> 00:02:16.100
where everything was happening
with like hip-hop and country.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.067
And it was like just something
magical happened

00:02:18.067 --> 00:02:21.133
at that Stagecoach festival, so
I hope to repeat it on Sunday.

00:02:21.133 --> 00:02:26.667
-And then, you also -- Can you
explain Burning Man to me?

00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:28.767
This is why you're here,
to help grandpa out.

00:02:28.767 --> 00:02:30.234
Thank you.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:30.234 --> 00:02:31.601
-I would love to take you
to Burning Man.

00:02:31.601 --> 00:02:34.667
It happens the end of August,
early September.

00:02:34.667 --> 00:02:37.167
And it's just -- it's a festival
like none other.

00:02:37.167 --> 00:02:39.300
You know, you can't really go
there and buy tickets.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:40.567
You kind of have to participate.

00:02:40.567 --> 00:02:43.133
Even as a deejay, I play like 20
different sets there

00:02:43.133 --> 00:02:45.901
and just enjoy the time
and look at the art.

00:02:45.901 --> 00:02:47.968
-You live there, right?
-You have to live there.

00:02:47.968 --> 00:02:50.567
You have to camp there for
the three, four, or five days.

00:02:50.567 --> 00:02:52.567
You might, like, run into,
like, Seth Rogen

00:02:52.567 --> 00:02:54.033
just smoking weed
in the middle of the desert.

00:02:54.033 --> 00:02:56.033
Just random.
It's super random who's there

00:02:56.033 --> 00:02:58.267
and you just kind of
enjoy the time there.

00:02:58.267 --> 00:03:01.367
It's so macro. Everything is
happening all the time.

00:03:01.367 --> 00:03:04.601
There's parties and art
and music and these art cars

00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:07.667
and like -- there's whole shower
camps and there's whole, like --

00:03:07.667 --> 00:03:10.100
-There's robots too, right?
-There's robots too.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:13.400
Yeah, Robot Heart actually is
the camp I play at sometimes.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:14.868
And some of the Robot Heart guys
are here today.

00:03:14.868 --> 00:03:16.200
So, shout-out, Robot Heart.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.934
-Robot Heart.
I'm interested in it.

00:03:19.934 --> 00:03:22.968
But I know that -- I was talking
to you quickly backstage.

00:03:22.968 --> 00:03:25.267
And I know you had to do this --
you did the sunrise.

00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:26.801
-I did the sunrise
at this last burn.

00:03:26.801 --> 00:03:28.467
It was kind of the renegade burn

00:03:28.467 --> 00:03:29.667
because there wasn't
the organization.

00:03:29.667 --> 00:03:31.067
You kind of had to just go out
there with --

00:03:31.067 --> 00:03:33.167
bring your own water
and survive in the desert.

00:03:33.167 --> 00:03:35.767
So, I happened to randomly be
in Reno.

00:03:35.767 --> 00:03:37.968
And the Robot Heart team hit me
up and said,

00:03:37.968 --> 00:03:40.000
"If you want to come out,
we can do a sunrise set,"

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.767
which is -- I'd say as a deejay,
it's the most coveted set.

00:03:42.767 --> 00:03:46.534
To do sunrise at Burning Man
is like the final boss,

00:03:46.534 --> 00:03:48.434
I think, for deejaying 'cause
you got to really get into it

00:03:48.434 --> 00:03:51.100
and do something really special,
really, really be magic.

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:53.968
And you can't just perform
your regular set.

00:03:53.968 --> 00:03:55.534
You have to really be in tune
with the audience

00:03:55.534 --> 00:03:57.934
and watch the sun rise
and that's your performance.

00:03:57.934 --> 00:03:59.934
It's like the sun's creeping
over the desert

00:03:59.934 --> 00:04:01.968
and people are there,
like, enjoying that.

00:04:01.968 --> 00:04:03.434
-And you got it
because you're Diplo,

00:04:03.434 --> 00:04:05.767
and they go, "Oh, this guy's a
trusted name. We know this guy."

00:04:05.767 --> 00:04:07.801
-No, it doesn't work at all
at Burning Man.

00:04:07.801 --> 00:04:09.467
It doesn't matter who you are.

00:04:09.467 --> 00:04:12.868
It's really about like --
It's literally another level.

00:04:12.868 --> 00:04:15.400
You can't have your brand

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.501
and what you've done in
your career stick out for you.

00:04:17.501 --> 00:04:19.000
You have to really be
part of this community.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.667
And I think I did
my first sunrise set,

00:04:21.667 --> 00:04:23.634
and it was just one of
my favorite sets of all time.

00:04:23.634 --> 00:04:25.133
And I happened to be there
randomly.

00:04:25.133 --> 00:04:28.167
I just was there for like three
hours and flew back to L.A.

00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:30.200
and didn't sleep
until that night.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:32.000
-Yeah, but how did you
get to do it?

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:33.434
How did you earn your way?

00:04:33.434 --> 00:04:34.634
-Well, you know,
it's kind of like --

00:04:34.634 --> 00:04:36.300
They give you like
a whole vision quest.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:37.834
Like, over couple different
Burning Mans,

00:04:37.834 --> 00:04:39.634
you have to do things like --
the year before, I had to take

00:04:39.634 --> 00:04:42.801
a big cow off the top of
the robot heart they had there

00:04:42.801 --> 00:04:44.000
and bring it down and --

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:46.767
-A cow climbed onto the top of
the robot heart?

00:04:46.767 --> 00:04:48.634
-For some reason,
the Robot Heart clique,

00:04:48.634 --> 00:04:50.968
they stole one of the cows
from Oktoberfest

00:04:50.968 --> 00:04:52.601
and they tied it
to the top of the heart.

00:04:52.601 --> 00:04:55.334
And I had to bring it down
because it was going to break.

00:04:55.334 --> 00:04:57.400
-But why is Diplo in charge of
removing the cow?

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:58.734
[ Laughter ]

00:04:58.734 --> 00:05:00.334
-They just saw I had a lot of
energy in the morning.

00:05:00.334 --> 00:05:02.901
I was on a little bit of LSD.
I was just ready to climb.

00:05:02.901 --> 00:05:05.334
-Okay, now, yeah.
Are you sure it was a cow?

00:05:05.334 --> 00:05:07.334
-Yeah, I think so.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:07.334 --> 00:05:09.501
There's some footage of it
somewhere.

00:05:09.501 --> 00:05:13.567
-What happens --
Do you take gigs like weddings

00:05:13.567 --> 00:05:15.868
and birthdays or bar mitzvahs?
-Everything.

00:05:15.868 --> 00:05:18.701
I mean, I'll take Stagecoach,
Burning Man, bar mitzvahs.

00:05:18.701 --> 00:05:20.400
I'm down for whatever.
-Yeah.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.767
And just because
you like deejaying

00:05:22.767 --> 00:05:23.968
and you know how to do it?

00:05:23.968 --> 00:05:25.734
-I love to deejay.
-You do?

00:05:25.734 --> 00:05:27.133
-I've been doing it,
you know, 20 years.

00:05:27.133 --> 00:05:30.200
I just love to, you know,
pick records and play records

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:31.667
for people and just bring music
to people.

00:05:31.667 --> 00:05:34.501
-You started with records,
right? Like, selling records.

00:05:34.501 --> 00:05:36.000
Or digging through crates
and then selling them?

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:37.968
-Back in Philadelphia. Yeah.
-Philly?

00:05:37.968 --> 00:05:39.667
Did you ever sell to Quest?

00:05:39.667 --> 00:05:42.033
-You know, a couple times,
we met up,

00:05:42.033 --> 00:05:43.767
and I'm not sure how it went,

00:05:43.767 --> 00:05:45.868
but we might have traded
records.

00:05:45.868 --> 00:05:47.501
But, I mean, I remember being
shoulder to shoulder with him

00:05:47.501 --> 00:05:49.767
and, like, having a fight for
space at record conventions

00:05:49.767 --> 00:05:51.534
'cause he was like
an animal out there.

00:05:51.534 --> 00:05:53.634
He was like --
It was like 20 years ago.

00:05:53.634 --> 00:05:55.868
-Yeah, how many albums do you
have now, Quest?

00:05:55.868 --> 00:05:58.934
-Eh, close to 200...thousand.

00:05:58.934 --> 00:06:00.634
[ Laughter ]

00:06:00.634 --> 00:06:03.100
-That's a lot. That's a lot.
-He has 200,000 --

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:05.133
Are you still in that same thing
where it just goes out

00:06:05.133 --> 00:06:06.734
and you have a ladder?
-Yeah, yeah.

00:06:06.734 --> 00:06:09.567
I have a sliding library.
-It's almost like a library.

00:06:09.567 --> 00:06:10.868
-This guy's on the level now.

00:06:10.868 --> 00:06:12.634
-Feels like a "Harry Potter"
library with a ladder

00:06:12.634 --> 00:06:13.501
that just moves automatically.

00:06:13.501 --> 00:06:14.934
You climb up and grab a record.

00:06:14.934 --> 00:06:16.701
-It's just an endless library
with just records, like,

00:06:16.701 --> 00:06:18.601
the whole four feet up.

00:06:18.601 --> 00:06:21.100
-Talk to me about this,
self-titled.

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:24.167
"Diplo," the name of the record.

00:06:24.167 --> 00:06:25.634
Are you excited about this?

00:06:25.634 --> 00:06:26.934
-I love it.

00:06:26.934 --> 00:06:28.834
I'm going to play a song for you
guys tonight with Miguel.

00:06:28.834 --> 00:06:31.267
-I love that you brought Miguel.
We love that.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:36.434
Just quickly,
I wanted to mention.

00:06:36.434 --> 00:06:39.300
I know last night -- You do --
Follow him on socials

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:41.868
if you want to follow someone
very interesting and fun, Diplo.

00:06:41.868 --> 00:06:45.067
You do things that I would never
do but in a great way.

00:06:45.067 --> 00:06:46.601
But exciting.

00:06:46.601 --> 00:06:50.100
For instance, yesterday was a
pop-up show you did in Brooklyn.

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:52.033
-Right, right.
-And you just go, "Yeah, hey,

00:06:52.033 --> 00:06:53.968
everybody, I'm going to perform
here, bang."

00:06:53.968 --> 00:06:56.501
And everyone showed up.
And then the cops shut it down.

00:06:56.501 --> 00:06:58.534
-The cops shut it down before we
even played one record.

00:06:58.534 --> 00:07:00.167
It was 4,000 kids in the park.

00:07:00.167 --> 00:07:02.133
And I forgot how posh Brooklyn
is now.

00:07:02.133 --> 00:07:04.200
Like, the cops come,
like, right away.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:05.501
-Yeah, they go,
"Yeah, we checked your --"

00:07:05.501 --> 00:07:07.334
Also you're on Instagram,
so the cops are like,

00:07:07.334 --> 00:07:08.667
"Okay, he's gonna be
in Brooklyn."

00:07:08.667 --> 00:07:10.834
So, I mean, you kind of gave it
away a little bit.

00:07:10.834 --> 00:07:11.834
-We had the kids out then.

00:07:11.834 --> 00:07:13.000
It was a guy who took over like

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:14.601
a little street party
and he got arrested.

00:07:14.601 --> 00:07:15.734
They took his equipment.

00:07:15.734 --> 00:07:17.734
This deejay's name is like
Subway Deejay.

00:07:17.734 --> 00:07:19.634
So I had to go and pay his --

00:07:19.634 --> 00:07:20.667
whatever his bail was
to get him out.

00:07:20.667 --> 00:07:21.868
-You paid his bail
to get him out?

00:07:21.868 --> 00:07:22.901
-Yeah. Saw the guy.
He was getting arrested

00:07:22.901 --> 00:07:24.133
right in front
of me on the street.

00:07:24.133 --> 00:07:25.400
And we were right in front of
Brooklyn Bowl

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:26.567
and we said, "Let's just move
the party here."

00:07:26.567 --> 00:07:29.033
So we asked the owners
and they let us in.

00:07:29.033 --> 00:07:31.133
-Brooklyn Bowl is the jam, dude.

00:07:31.133 --> 00:07:34.300
-It was a big party.
-See, Brooklyn's still got it.

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:36.400
Brooklyn's still got it.
Diplo, I love you.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.067
Thanks for being here, buddy,
and thanks for performing.

00:07:39.067 --> 00:07:41.734
Diplo, everybody! Check out
his new album, "Diplo,"

00:07:41.734 --> 00:07:42.901
which is out right now.

00:07:42.901 --> 00:07:44.868
He's performing with Miguel
later in show.

00:07:44.868 --> 00:07:47.133
More "Tonight Show" after the
break, everybody. Come on back.

